1
00:00:02,560 --> 00:00:11,040
So here's a question that people commonly ask:\h
"Why is stream entry called the right view,

2
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:19,280
and why can a change of view be so special that it\h
becomes actually liberating? Don't most Buddhists

3
00:00:19,280 --> 00:00:26,329
already have right view in the sense that their\h
views are in line with the Buddhist teaching?"

4
00:00:30,240 --> 00:00:38,080
Well, the thing is that, as the suttas say, 
there are two factors to the

5
00:00:38,080 --> 00:00:45,680
arising of the right view. So on a mundane\h
level, all you need to change your view about a

6
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:51,280
topic is the utterance of another. You just need\h
to get information and then you just change

7
00:00:51,280 --> 00:00:58,400
your mind about, I don't know, politics or\h
something. But when it comes to the dhamma,

8
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:04,960
there's that second factor there that is also\h
essential for that change of view to occur, which

9
00:01:04,960 --> 00:01:15,200
is yoniso manasikāra. So then basically it's not a change\h
of view on that kind of arms length level where I

10
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:22,160
just exchange one bit of information that I agree\h
with for another bit of information which, as you\hsaid,

11
00:01:22,160 --> 00:01:26,160
that's pretty much most people's\h
relationship with the Buddhist teaching;

12
00:01:26,160 --> 00:01:37,760
it's just agreeing with a different information than\h
they had before. But this is like, yoniso manasikāra being,

13
00:01:37,760 --> 00:01:46,013
as some other suttas describe it, it's inseparable\h
from seeing your own mind, seeing the signs of your\hown mind.

14
00:01:46,013 --> 00:01:55,840
So when you take the Buddhist teaching\h
in its most condensed form, which is suffering,

15
00:01:55,840 --> 00:02:01,520
the origin of suffering, the escape from suffering,\h
and the path to the escape from suffering,

16
00:02:01,520 --> 00:02:06,320
you would realize that if you don't see your\h
own mind, any information that you might have

17
00:02:06,320 --> 00:02:15,040
about this, how will it possibly be personal? 
How will it possibly be relevant to you in

18
00:02:15,040 --> 00:02:20,640
your own situation? As the suttas say, the dhamma\h
has to be realized by the wise for themselves.

19
00:02:20,640 --> 00:02:30,720
So if there isn't that kind of ability to\h
even see where this stuff is supposed to apply,

20
00:02:30,720 --> 00:02:36,720
you can have all the information you want, but\h
your view about where suffering comes from will

21
00:02:36,720 --> 00:02:43,760
still be conditioned by the extent of awareness of\h
your own mind that you still have, which for most

22
00:02:43,760 --> 00:02:53,440
people is basically zero. So you will not be able\h
to clearly see what craving is for example.

23
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:59,280
You might have all sorts of ideas about it, but those\h
ideas will necessarily be wrong if you haven't

24
00:02:59,280 --> 00:03:06,160
developed that internal room or space let's say\h
to see where the craving actually arises on the

25
00:03:06,160 --> 00:03:13,200
right level. And what is that space? Well, it's\h
again, anything you might say is just going to be

26
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:21,920
more information; it's what gets revealed on its\h
own provided you make the right effort.

27
00:03:21,920 --> 00:03:29,440
Towards what? Towards virtue, sense restraint, not letting\h
yourself spill out towards external objects that

28
00:03:29,440 --> 00:03:35,120
will obscure your mind even more. So it's not\h
that these things will inherently reveal your

29
00:03:35,120 --> 00:03:41,120
mind, because you can be keeping the precepts\h
and restraining your senses for all sorts of

30
00:03:41,120 --> 00:03:47,120
wrong reasons and many people do that today with\h
no insight coming from it whatsoever, but it's

31
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:54,480
a indispensable prerequisite for you to be able\h
to see where your mind actually is. So, it's kind

32
00:03:54,480 --> 00:04:03,920
of like, you know, you have to remove\h
the stuff that's in the way.

33
00:04:03,920 --> 00:04:10,240
Like there's a whole tree that fell on the way that you\h
have to walk. So, it's not that removing the tree

34
00:04:10,240 --> 00:04:16,240
in and of itself puts you at your destination, but\h
if the tree is there, you will not be able to walk\hthrough.

35
00:04:16,240 --> 00:04:20,880
So that's why, as the Buddha\h
said, virtue is not the dhamma, but there is no

36
00:04:20,880 --> 00:04:28,560
dhamma without virtue. So if you develop these\h
prerequisites, what will you start to see?

37
00:04:28,560 --> 00:04:34,979
Well, in the process of even developing them, you\h
will come to see that there is this

38
00:04:34,979 --> 00:04:43,120
separate dimension; not that it didn't exist\h
before, but that you always overlooked.

39
00:04:43,120 --> 00:04:50,880
You were always either landing too much on the side\h
of the sense objects and sight, sounds, smells,

40
00:04:50,880 --> 00:05:01,440
taste, touches, or kind of like semi-mystical\h
grandiose ideas about how everything works and

41
00:05:01,440 --> 00:05:08,960
explanatory concepts and motions\h
to kind of help yourself deal with the pressure of

42
00:05:08,960 --> 00:05:13,680
sight, sounds, smells, tastes, and touches. 
So those are\hthe two dimensions that a person

43
00:05:13,680 --> 00:05:23,280
would know. And then what's there kind of neither\h
in one nor the other? It's the citta.

44
00:05:23,280 --> 00:05:30,080
It's that attitude at the root level\h
of your experience that you cannot directly see,

45
00:05:30,080 --> 00:05:42,320
you can only indirectly know. You have this kind\h
of agitation, obsession; one day it's there,

46
00:05:42,320 --> 00:05:48,000
one day it's not there, the other day it's kind\h
of revolving around this types of thoughts,

47
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:52,720
the other day a different types of thoughts. 
And so it's that kind of

48
00:05:52,720 --> 00:06:03,360
ambiguous, indirect nature of the mind that\h
the only way to reveal it is by ceasing to

49
00:06:03,360 --> 00:06:09,040
let yourself spill out into sense objects. And\h
then the other prerequisite, as the Buddha said, is

50
00:06:09,040 --> 00:06:14,880
to not delight in company, even if you're\h
keeping the precepts, because then that will

51
00:06:14,880 --> 00:06:20,080
do the opposite; that will keep spilling\h
you out into the world, into resolving

52
00:06:20,080 --> 00:06:27,440
things on the level of getting people to\h
agree with you, to not disapprove of you,

53
00:06:27,440 --> 00:06:36,400
to do things as a group. And all of\h
that is actually obscuring that internal, fully

54
00:06:36,400 --> 00:06:44,160
individual and personal dimension of where your\h
own craving actually arises.

55
00:06:44,160 --> 00:06:49,440
Because you take "external" now as a starting point again.\h
So you would be attending to the wrong signs

56
00:06:49,440 --> 00:06:54,480
and features, namely what do other people think of\h
me?, how do they look? and so on. Whereas all of that

57
00:06:54,480 --> 00:07:00,560
actually means nothing compared to the real signs\h
and features, which cannot be seen

58
00:07:00,560 --> 00:07:05,200
on the outside. It's your own privileged position\h
in relation to your own mind.

59
00:07:05,200 --> 00:07:11,520
Yes, and also, it's not that other people
 inherently make you

60
00:07:11,520 --> 00:07:16,560
take the external standpoint. It's that you\h
already start with the external standpoint.

61
00:07:16,560 --> 00:07:22,480
So then other people will just keep reinforcing\h
that. If you don't keep it in check

62
00:07:22,480 --> 00:07:27,600
and especially if you keep seeking it out, which is\h
the problem. It's not that you have to avoid other

63
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:34,000
people and run away from them. It's if you delight\h
in company. In other words, if you use that as

64
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:40,880
as a thing to kind of keep yourself afloat when\h
you're bored or something. Well, that's a perfect\hexample:

65
00:07:40,880 --> 00:07:47,760
if you were not to seek company\h
as a means of enjoyment and pleasure, what would

66
00:07:47,760 --> 00:07:56,240
you be forced to encounter and face? It's that\h
full extent of your mind's pressure towards this.

67
00:07:56,240 --> 00:08:04,305
And then you would in the position
to unravel what's actually happening,

68
00:08:04,305 --> 00:08:10,960
why is it that this is painful,\h
not to have this company? Why?

69
00:08:10,960 --> 00:08:16,320
That could manifest as a sense of loneliness\h
for example. Exactly. But then, you know,

70
00:08:16,320 --> 00:08:24,800
it doesn't need to come to the point of you having\h
the view that other people is how you resolve your

71
00:08:24,800 --> 00:08:30,320
suffering, that other people are the escape from\h
suffering. Because, again, it's not the view

